Wow, this was a great run. I've never had this much fun with a Rogue
before.

The Good Part, (a little long and spoily)

I started off the game with a co-aligned altar right next to the
upstairs,, but I avoided the risky (and pretty much always fatal in my
experience) temptation of an early sac-fest in order to procure more
armor/food/etc. I let my dog do most the killing for the first few
levels, keeping the experience level of the monster spawns in the slow
and weak range. I only picked off monsters I couldn't avoid and always
by throwing daggers. In this manner, my little dog promoted its way to
dog in three or four levels.

DLVL2 had a huge food shop with more rations than I bothered to count.
Food was not at all an issue, even without this shop. I closed the shop
and locked it to keep my dog out for the time being.

Picked up and tried on all safe armor. An early find of plate mail
brought my AC down toward zero. A short while later, I ditched it for a
set of banded mail, deciding a 1 reduction in AC was offset by the
ability to carry more loot. This could have gone either way for me,
really, but the generous donation of a cloak of protection by a hapless
halfling made the AC reduction worth the risk. I found an early can of
grease and proceeded to grease all my armor thuroughly. I also found a
pair of boots that served me nicely. I'm sure I had an orcish helm
early on, seeing as those things are handed out like crackerjack prizes
(YAI: Box of Crackerjack. contains a random tool). I must have had an
AC of -1 or -2 before Sokoban.

Ditched my short sword for a wielded elven dagger. Turned out to be
blessed and enchanted,
so it was an extra good move.

The stairs Gnomish Mines were on DLVL4. GM1 was unlit so I decided to
save my puny XL1 rogue the embarrassment of a quick death. I forged
ahead to Sokoban.

Killing a floating eye finally brought me up XL2 and granted me
telepathy. At this point I decided an early protection run was out. I
didn't have anywhere near enough gold yet so the prospects of
succeeding were slim. I decided practicing with my dagger was the way
to go: "kill them before they can reach you, and you don't even need
AC."

Sokoban was pretty uneventful. A dwarf left me +1 dwarven boots in his
will, knocking my AC down a little lower. I got all the easy levels, so
the run went fast. My dog became a large dog, and I advanced dagger to
Expert with no trouble. I'd started with 13 orcish daggers, which was a
great number to have: enough to kill anything I faced without emptying
my quiver. I machine gunned my way through Sokoban, Did the treasure
zoo, and came out with a BoH. The only risk came from a couple ant
attacks. Even the mimics were pretty easy pickings.

Left Sokoban, ran back to the mines, and descended to Mine Town. A
dwarf donated a plus +3 dwarven helm The temple was cross-aligned, but
helped me begin my identification at least.

Shops were fairly meager in their offerings. A quick scan of weapons
spoilers indicated that rogues can enhance #twoweapon to Expert.
Mwahahaa. #Twoweaponing a blessed elven dagger and a blessed dagger.
With dagger already at Expert, meleeing while #twoweaponed proved easy.


Large Dog took out most of the guards one by one. Finally it attacked
the Mine Captain, and I knew the dog wouldn't survive without help.
What's an orc to do? One pincussioned Watch Captain later, I had a new
pair of gloves (the only pair I'd seen in game to this point), an AC of
-8 without protection, a silver saber, and a newfound respect for the
chaotic alignment.

The Watch Captain had crystal plate, but the Dwarven Mithril meant I
could go unburdened without leaving a huge stash. I shattered the plate
with a swift kick to make sure no other monsters would get to use it.

Torn between getting the luck stone and heading back to DLVL1 to do a
sac fest, I decided to get the luckstone first.

This is where things began to get crazy.

The level below mine town, my dog fell through a trap door. I went down
and found him again only to lose him to a level teleporter. Reasoning I
was closer to the bottom at this point, I decided to go down to search
for him, then if I couldn't find him, quickly come back up. I went
quickly from level to level, using telepathy to check for my pet. The
second to last level of the minds had a master mindflayer. Unlucky. I
didn't have to face it, so I just took the stairs down and left it in
its discussion with a gnome king. That placed me in the catacombs,
surrounded by undead. I took a brief pounding as I did a quick look for
my dog. Nada. Ran back upstairs to the MMF level and a hungry V
followed me. Whacked it until it teleported away,  then mindful of the
mindflayer, headed for the upstairs. I had my blindfold on to watch for
the MMF and Vampire, which is why I  ran straight into an air
elemental. Whack. Whack. Whack. #pray. Whack. Whack. Whack. It died
before I did. Just barely. After that, the cockatrice guarding the
upstairs seemed a joke.

On the next level, five gnomes I'd ignored took turns jumping into a
polytrap. Fought an ogreking and a red dragon. Somehow, I managed to
not die.

I climbed all the way up out of the mines, then headed back toward DLVL
1. Found Fido on DLVL2, took him upstairs to the altar and  locked him
in a room while I used three wands of create monster in an attempt to
earn an artifact. Kos didn't feel I needed one at this time. I settled
for a water prayer. Blessed my BoH. Kept three more vials of holy water
in reserve, then Fido and I ran back down into the mines. I could not
convince him to step on the poly trap without a whistle or leash, so we
went down another level.

This is where the YASD comes in.

There was a magic trap on this level in a long corridor, I'd snuck past
it before, but this time I stepped in it. ZAP. I'm blinded, with a rust
monster and a troll next to me. I let the rust monster chew on my
daggers a bit, leaving four of them rusty and the rust monster dead. I
then stepped backward and the troll followed onto the trap square. I
killed it and stupidly, stepped forward again, thinking I needed to get
rid of the corpse.

ZAP. This time I hac a cockatrice on one side and a Leucrotta on the
other. Panicking, I tried to dispatch the 'trice, forgetting just how
nasty Leucrotta's attacks are. DYWYPI?

Mistake #1: Stepping on a magic trap in the first place.
Mistake #2: Stepping on it again. (Unforgivable)
Mistake #3: Panicking and attempting to Valk Smash my way out of a
sticky situation with a rogue. A quick inventory check would have
revealed a scroll of teleport that would have saved me.

My next Rogue will be more careful.

Comments and suggestions are welcome.



